      <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-semibold bg-primary/10 border border-primary/20 text-primary">
        <span>Geometric reference and calculation principles</span>
      </div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-ink tracking-tight">
        Geometric principles and volume measurement standards
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-ink-secondary">
        Calculating three-dimensional space accurately requires applying precise mathematical equations to clean physical measurements. Whether you calculate cargo volume for freight shipping, fluid capacity for storage tanks, or structural displacement for building foundations, geometric equations determine spatial capacity. The calculations on this site run in 64-bit floating-point precision, converting linear dimensions into cubic volume and liquid capacity across metric and imperial systems.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-ink tracking-tight flex items-center gap-2">
        <span class="w-2 h-6 rounded-full bg-primary inline-block"></span>
        <span>Definition of volume and measurement units</span>
      </h3>
      <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-ink-secondary">
        In physics and geometry, volume is the total quantity of three-dimensional space enclosed by a boundary or occupied by a substance. Surface area covers an exterior boundary in square units, such as square meters or square feet. Volume measures three linear dimensions at once, producing cubic units such as cubic meters, cubic feet, cubic centimeters, or liquid capacity units such as litres and gallons.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-ink-secondary">
        To calculate volume for any standard solid, measure its linear dimensions and apply the mathematical equation for that shape. For polyhedra such as cubes and boxes, volume is the area of the base multiplied by perpendicular height. For circular shapes such as cylinders and spheres, the mathematical constant Pi (π ≈ 3.141592653589793) accounts for boundary curvature. Entering measurements into the calculator provides exact numerical results and formula breakdowns.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-ink tracking-tight flex items-center gap-2">
        <span class="w-2 h-6 rounded-full bg-primary inline-block"></span>
        <span>Standard volume formulas for 3D geometric shapes</span>
      </h3>
      <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-ink-secondary">
        Our multi-shape calculator supports sixteen distinct geometries. Each shape uses a verified equation:
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 pt-2">
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline space-y-2">
          <div class="font-bold text-sm text-ink flex items-center justify-between">
            <span>Rectangular box</span>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono bg-primary/10 text-primary px-2 py-0.5 rounded">V = l × w × h</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted">
            Multiply length by width by height. This formula applies to cardboard packages, rooms, and shipping cartons.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline space-y-2">
          <div class="font-bold text-sm text-ink flex items-center justify-between">
            <span>Cube</span>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono bg-primary/10 text-primary px-2 py-0.5 rounded">V = s³</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted">
            All edges of a cube have identical length. Multiply the single edge measurement by itself three times.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline space-y-2">
          <div class="font-bold text-sm text-ink flex items-center justify-between">
            <a href="/cylinder-volume-calculator" class="hover:text-primary transition-colors underline decoration-hairline hover:decoration-primary">Cylinder volume</a>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono bg-primary/10 text-primary px-2 py-0.5 rounded">V = π × r² × h</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted">
            Multiply circular base area by vertical height. Used for water storage tanks, metal pipes, and industrial barrels.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline space-y-2">
          <div class="font-bold text-sm text-ink flex items-center justify-between">
            <a href="/sphere-volume-calculator" class="hover:text-primary transition-colors underline decoration-hairline hover:decoration-primary">Sphere formula</a>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono bg-primary/10 text-primary px-2 py-0.5 rounded">V = (4/3) × π × r³</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted">
            Multiply four-thirds by Pi and the cube of the radius. This equation applies to sports balls, storage globes, and spherical tanks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline space-y-2">
          <div class="font-bold text-sm text-ink flex items-center justify-between">
            <span>Cone</span>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono bg-primary/10 text-primary px-2 py-0.5 rounded">V = (1/3) × π × r² × h</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted">
            Multiply one-third by Pi, base radius squared, and vertical height. Standard for funnels, silos, and hoppers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline space-y-2">
          <div class="font-bold text-sm text-ink flex items-center justify-between">
            <a href="/square-pyramid-volume-calculator" class="hover:text-primary transition-colors underline decoration-hairline hover:decoration-primary">Pyramid volume formula</a>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono bg-primary/10 text-primary px-2 py-0.5 rounded">V = (1/3) × a² × h</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted">
            Multiply one-third by base edge squared and vertical height. Solves capacity for pyramid roofs, monuments, and hoppers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline space-y-2 md:col-span-2">
          <div class="font-bold text-sm text-ink flex items-center justify-between">
            <span>Horizontal cylinder tank</span>
            <span class="text-xs font-mono bg-primary/10 text-primary px-2 py-0.5 rounded">Partial dip-stick calculus</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted">
            Uses circular segment integration based on liquid fill depth and tank length to calculate remaining fuel or water.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-ink tracking-tight flex items-center gap-2">
        <span class="w-2 h-6 rounded-full bg-accent-teal inline-block"></span>
        <span>Liquid capacity and water volume calculations</span>
      </h3>
      <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-ink-secondary">
        Homeowners and facility managers calculate volume when sizing swimming pools, garden ponds, rainwater collection barrels, and hot tubs. Liquid containers are usually labeled in gallons or litres rather than cubic inches.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-ink-secondary">
        Linear measurements convert directly into fluid capacity. For instance, a cylindrical garden pool with a diameter of 12 feet and a water depth of 3 feet has a cubic volume of 339.29 cubic feet. Because one cubic foot of fresh water contains 7.48052 US liquid gallons according to NIST Special Publication 811, the pool holds approximately 2,538 gallons of water, which equals 9,607 litres.
      </p>
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-accent-teal/5 border border-accent-teal/20 space-y-2">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-sm text-ink">Liquid conversion factors (NIST SP 811 standard)</h4>
        <ul class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-ink-secondary list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>1 cubic foot equals 7.48052 US liquid gallons, 6.2288 Imperial gallons, and 28.3168 litres</li>
          <li>1 cubic meter equals 1,000 litres, 264.172 US liquid gallons, and 219.969 Imperial gallons</li>
          <li>1 US gallon equals 231 cubic inches, 3.78541 litres, and 0.13368 cubic feet</li>
          <li>1 Imperial gallon equals 4.54609 litres and 1.20095 US gallons</li>
          <li>1 litre of fresh water has a mass of 1 kilogram; 1 US gallon weighs approximately 8.34 pounds</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-ink tracking-tight flex items-center gap-2">
        <span class="w-2 h-6 rounded-full bg-accent-orange inline-block"></span>
        <span>Cubic feet measurements in shipping and construction</span>
      </h3>
      <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-ink-secondary">
        In North America, cubic feet are standard for shipping packages, renting storage lockers, and purchasing building materials. When packing a trailer or booking freight, calculating cubic feet prevents carrier surcharges and clarifies capacity requirements.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-ink-secondary">
        Rulers and tape measures mark dimensions in inches. Because one linear foot equals 12 inches, one cubic foot equals 12 inches multiplied by 12 inches multiplied by 12 inches, which equals 1,728 cubic inches. To calculate cubic feet from measurements in inches, multiply length, width, and height, then divide by 1,728:
      </p>
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-center font-bold text-primary">
        Cubic feet = [Length (in) × Width (in) × Height (in)] ÷ 1,728
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-ink-secondary">
        In landscaping and concrete pouring, suppliers sell materials by the cubic yard. One cubic yard equals 27 cubic feet (3 ft × 3 ft × 3 ft). If an excavation or garden bed requires 81 cubic feet of soil, divide by 27 to find that you need 3 cubic yards of material.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-ink tracking-tight flex items-center gap-2">
        <span class="w-2 h-6 rounded-full bg-primary inline-block"></span>
        <span>Metric volume scale: cubic meters, litres, and cubic millimeters</span>
      </h3>
      <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-ink-secondary">
        The metric system simplifies three-dimensional calculations because its units scale in decimal multiples of ten. The fundamental SI unit of volume is the cubic meter (m³).
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-ink-secondary">
        One cubic meter equals 1,000 litres, 1,000,000 cubic centimeters, and 1,000,000,000 cubic millimeters. Freight forwarders quote international container space in CBM (cubic meters). A standard 20-foot shipping container holds roughly 33 CBM of cargo, while a 40-foot container accommodates roughly 67 CBM.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-ink tracking-tight flex items-center gap-2">
        <span class="w-2 h-6 rounded-full bg-primary inline-block"></span>
        <span>Volume unit conversion reference table (NIST SP 811 & ISO 80000-3)</span>
      </h3>
      <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-ink-secondary">
        This table displays direct conversion values across metric, imperial, and fluid volume units based on standards published in NIST Special Publication 811 and ISO 80000-3:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-hairline">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-xs sm:text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-canvas-soft border-b border-hairline text-ink font-bold">
            <tr>
              <th class="p-3">Unit</th>
              <th class="p-3">Symbol</th>
              <th class="p-3">Litres (L)</th>
              <th class="p-3">Cubic feet (ft³)</th>
              <th class="p-3">US gallons (gal)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline text-ink-secondary">
            <tr class="hover:bg-canvas-soft/40">
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-ink">Cubic meter</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">m³</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">1,000 L</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">35.3147 ft³</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">264.172 gal</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="hover:bg-canvas-soft/40">
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-ink">Litre</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">L</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">1 L</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">0.035315 ft³</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">0.264172 gal</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="hover:bg-canvas-soft/40">
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-ink">US liquid gallon</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">gal</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">3.78541 L</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">0.133681 ft³</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">1 gal</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="hover:bg-canvas-soft/40">
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-ink">Cubic foot</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">ft³</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">28.3168 L</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">1 ft³</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">7.48052 gal</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="hover:bg-canvas-soft/40">
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-ink">Cubic yard</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">yd³</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">764.555 L</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">27 ft³</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">201.974 gal</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="hover:bg-canvas-soft/40">
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-ink">Cubic inch</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">in³</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">0.016387 L</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">0.0005787 ft³</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">0.004329 gal</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="hover:bg-canvas-soft/40">
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-ink">Cubic millimeter</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">mm³</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">0.000001 L</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">3.5315 × 10⁻⁸ ft³</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">2.6417 × 10⁻⁷ gal</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-ink tracking-tight flex items-center gap-2">
        <span class="w-2 h-6 rounded-full bg-primary inline-block"></span>
        <span>Everyday applications of volume calculations</span>
      </h3>
      <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-ink-secondary">
        Precise calculations prevent costly errors across domestic, commercial, and engineering tasks:
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-1">
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft/50 space-y-2">
          <div class="font-bold text-sm text-ink">Freight and shipping</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">
            Courier companies bill using dimensional weight, dividing total cubic inches by a dimensional divisor. Calculating package volume accurately prevents re-billing adjustments.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft/50 space-y-2">
          <div class="font-bold text-sm text-ink">Water and pool treatment</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">
            Chlorine dosages, pH balancers, and algaecides require exact liquid measurements. Incorrect volume calculations risk chemical imbalances or wasted treatments.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft/50 space-y-2">
          <div class="font-bold text-sm text-ink">Heating and cooling sizing</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">
            Heating and ventilation systems require capacity ratings based on the total enclosed air volume of a structure, including vaulted ceilings and open halls.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </article>

  <section id="faq-section" class="bg-white dark:bg-canvas-card rounded-2xl border border-hairline p-6 sm:p-10 shadow-xs space-y-6">
    <div class="border-b border-hairline pb-4">
      <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-semibold bg-primary/10 border border-primary/20 text-primary mb-2">
        <span>Authoritative answers</span>
      </div>
      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink tracking-tight">
        Frequently asked questions about volume
      </h2>
      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-ink-muted mt-1">
        Mathematical derivations and conversion answers for common volumetric questions.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-3.5">
      {HOME_FAQS.map((faq, index) => (
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-hairline p-4 sm:p-5 transition-all open:bg-canvas-soft/40 open:border-primary/40">
          <summary class="font-bold text-sm sm:text-base text-ink cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between gap-3">
            <span class="flex items-center gap-2.5">
              <span class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-primary/10 text-primary text-xs font-bold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0">
                {index + 1}
              </span>
              <span>{faq.question}</span>
            </span>
            <span class="text-ink-muted transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180 text-xs shrink-0">
              ▼
            </span>
          </summary>
          <div class="mt-3 pt-3 border-t border-hairline text-xs sm:text-sm text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed pl-8 sm:pl-8.5">
            {faq.answer}
          </div>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
</section>
